WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.400
What in the Super Mario world does a '90s&nbsp;
"Godzilla" flick have to do with the new "Mario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.134
Brothers" movie? It's either a Giant Land-sized&nbsp;
Easter egg, or just a goofy coincidence.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.640
"The Super Mario Bros. Movie"&nbsp;
keeps things relatively grounded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.760
or at least as grounded as a&nbsp;
story about a pair of Brooklyn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.780
plumbers transported to a magical land&nbsp;
called the "Mushroom Kingdom" can be.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.700
As you'd expect with family-friendly fare,&nbsp;
it's a fairly straightforward story. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.320
evil Bowser wants to marry Princess Peach&nbsp;
and take over the world, so Mario and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.180
new friends set out to stop him. But there are&nbsp;
plenty of wild places for a follow-up to go.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.800
In the games, Mario has traveled across&nbsp;
the galaxy, explored strange new lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:46.800
and even used a magical cap to mind-control a&nbsp;
T-Rex. The sky's the limit as far as sequels are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.000
concerned, and the movie offers some hints&nbsp;
as to where Mario and Luigi could go next.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.660
One of these clues lies in the&nbsp;
post-credits scene. After Mario and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.020
his team have defeated Bowser and saved&nbsp;
both Brooklyn and the Mushroom Kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.100
the brothers go about living&nbsp;
in this strange new world.

00:01:01.132 --> 00:01:03.360
But they may not be done with their old home yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.260
as the scene takes viewers through the&nbsp;
underground before landing on a green-spotted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:12.120
egg that clearly belongs to none other than&nbsp;
Mario's long-time dinosaur partner, Yoshi.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.100
It's not the first Yoshi hint to pop up in the&nbsp;
film. When Mario, Peach, and Toad travel across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.300
various lands to reach the Jungle Kingdom,&nbsp;
they pass a whole herd of roaming Yoshis.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.320
However, the one in the egg at the end&nbsp;
of the film almost certainly belongs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:29.880
the green Yoshi most closely associated&nbsp;
with Mario. It's a clear teaser for Mario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.320
eventually teaming up with Yoshi in the&nbsp;
sequel. But not-so-clear is why the "Super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.620
Mario Bros. Movie" post-credits scene appears to&nbsp;
be referencing 1998's "Godzilla," of all films.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.547
"He looks angry."

00:01:44.547 --> 00:01:46.324
Yep, you heard that right. &nbsp;

00:01:46.324 --> 00:01:51.300
At the&nbsp;conclusion of that widely-panned flick, after&nbsp;
humanity has defeated the giant monster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.660
the camera guides the viewer through&nbsp;
the ruins of Madison Square Garden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.660 --> 00:02:00.749
landing at one of Godzilla's eggs. It hatches, and&nbsp;
a baby Godzilla emerges, roaring at the screen.

00:02:00.749 --> 00:02:04.589
The "Super Mario Bros. Movie"&nbsp;
post-credits scene is weirdly similar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.589 --> 00:02:08.040
down to the camera movement, but&nbsp;
there's no hatching. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.360
as the screen cuts to black, Yoshi does&nbsp;
emit his signature high-pitched squeak.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.920
Yoshi's inclusion at the end of "The Super Mario&nbsp;
Bros. Movie" could hint at where a sequel could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.660
head. Yoshi became Mario's companion in&nbsp;
"Super Mario World," where Mario and Luigi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:27.240
go on a vacation to Dinosaur Land with Princess&nbsp;
Toadstool, who is (of course) kidnapped by Bowser.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.500
The baby Godzilla hatching from its egg was&nbsp;
actually supposed to set up a sequel to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.840
monster flick. In fact, 1998's "Godzilla" was&nbsp;
supposed to set up an entire trilogy, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:40.980
the second installment following that Godzilla&nbsp;
growing up and forming an attachment to Matthew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.780
Broderick's character from the first film, causing&nbsp;
him to track the creature to the South Pacific.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.040
Additionally, that baby Godzilla would've&nbsp;
been all grown up with a brood of its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:55.260
wreaking all sorts of havoc. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
the negative critical reaction to the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.000
"Godzilla" made Sony wary of pursuing&nbsp;
a follow-up, and it never materialized.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.440
Hopefully, "The Super Mario Bros. Movie" doesn't&nbsp;
have the same issue. While the new "Mario"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.829
movie received mixed reviews from critics, it's&nbsp;
expected to do extremely well at the box office.

00:03:09.829 --> 00:03:15.480
In fact, it's projected to make roughly $225&nbsp;
million worldwide in its opening weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:20.100
and if the numbers hold steady, it shouldn't&nbsp;
be long until Mario's off on another big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.240
screen adventure, this time with the&nbsp;
help of his friendly dinosaur steed.

